{"data":{"status":true,"competition":{"id":1765054,"visibility":"public","theme_colour":"blue-dark_un","paid":0,"title":"RoboCannonade","festival_id":null,"organization_id":2084580,"web_url":null,"start_date":"2026-10-02T00:00:00+05:30","end_date":"2026-10-31T17:00:00+05:30","location":null,"type":"competitions","public_url":"competitions\/robocannonade-mechanical-engineering-society-mes-national-institute-of-technology-warangal-nitw-1765054","region":"offline","details":"<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>RoboCannonade: Technozion, NIT Warangal<\/strong><\/p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>About the Event<\/strong><br \/>RoboCannonade is a hands-on robotics competition where teams design and build a stationary, multi-axis launcher that fires table tennis balls at randomized targets of varying depths and heights. The challenge combines mechanical precision with software control. Teams can run in remote-controlled or autonomous mode, and autonomy earns bonus points. Entries are judged on accuracy, consistency and engineering design.<\/p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Eligibility<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul dir=\"ltr\">\n<li>Open to students from colleges and universities across India.<\/li>\n<li>Team size: 3 to 5 members.<\/li>\n<li>Participants must be present at NIT Warangal for the event.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Format and Process<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul dir=\"ltr\">\n<li><strong>Concept and design report\/plan:<\/strong> submit by <strong>20 October<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Video submission of the build:<\/strong> submit by <strong>25 October<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Final event at NITW (during Technozion):<\/strong> teams compete on the arena.\n<ul dir=\"ltr\">\n<li>Setup time: strictly 3 minutes.<\/li>\n<li>Firing time: 3 to 5 minutes from the start whistle.<\/li>\n<li>Attempts: 10 shots per team.<\/li>\n<li>Targets are placed randomly.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Challenge \/ Problem Statement<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul dir=\"ltr\">\n<li>Build a completely stationary, non-mobile launcher.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Projectile:<\/strong> standard 40mm (or 40+ mm) table tennis balls weighing 2.7 g, provided by the teams.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Power:<\/strong> onboard batteries, or a regulated external DC adapter capped at 24V DC.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Size:<\/strong> the robot must fit within a 50 cm &times; 50 cm &times; 50 cm starting volume. It may expand after the start.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Mechanism:<\/strong> minimum 3 degrees of freedom (DOF).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Targets:<\/strong> placed at varying depths, distances and heights, from 1 m to 7 m from the robot's starting line.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Control:<\/strong> remote or autonomous, at the team's choice.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Scoring<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul dir=\"ltr\">\n<li>Base points: +20 (1&ndash;2 m), +40 (2&ndash;4 m), +80 (4 m and beyond).<\/li>\n<li>Multiplier: 1.5&times; for extreme heights or obstructed depths.<\/li>\n<li>Autonomy bonus: up to 250 points for a completely autonomous run.<\/li>\n<li>Secondary evaluation: near misses, software\/algorithmic performance, cost-efficiency and mechanical innovation.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Judging Criteria<\/strong><br \/>Accuracy, consistency and engineering design.<\/p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Rules<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul dir=\"ltr\">\n<li>The robot must remain stationary throughout the run.<\/li>\n<li>Power must stay within the 24V DC limit.<\/li>\n<li>The robot must fit the 50 cm cube at the start.<\/li>\n<li>Teams must stay behind safety lines and must not fire before the whistle.<\/li>\n<li>Teams must not interfere with the arena or other teams, or damage the arena.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Prizes<\/strong><br \/>The exact prize pool will be announced later.<\/p>","overall_prizes":"Total prize pool of \u20b925,000 for the top-performing teams, plus certificates.","regn_open":1,"regn_type":1,"regn_url":null,"regn_email":"","registration_clicks":0,"user_id":2665436,"moderation_stat